{%- comment -%}
  SHUDHI — Recent Journal

  The band on the product page between the FAQ and Customer Reviews: an
  editorial masthead rule (eyebrow, title, lede, "all entries" link) over a grid
  of recent article cards. Styled in assets/shudhi-journal.css.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  SEVEN THINGS HERE ARE LOAD-BEARING
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  1. THE ROOT CARRIES `sh`. assets/shudhi-home.js:300 selects `.sh [data-reveal]`
     and adds .is-in; the CSS pre-state (shudhi-home.css:234) starts those
     elements at opacity 0. Without the bare class every card stays invisible for
     the life of the page. Same rule as sh-faq.liquid note 2 and sh-reviews note 1.

  2. IT IS [data-reveal], NOT [data-sh-reveal]. Two motion systems exist in this
     theme. The story/ritual sections use [data-sh-reveal], driven by
     assets/sh-story-motion.js — and NOTHING on templates/product.json loads that
     file. Picking the wrong attribute here is the silent opacity-0 bug.

  3. THE COUNT IS COUNTED, AND IT PICKS THE GRID. `shown` is the real number of
     cards about to render, capped by the `count` setting, and it writes
     --n1/--n2 onto the root. Three columns is the CSS ceiling; with the two
     articles this store publishes today, --n2 gives two half-width feature
     cards instead of two thirds of a row and a column of dead cream.

  4. THE BAND SELF-SUPPRESSES WHEN THERE IS NOTHING TO SHOW. A blog with zero
     articles — or a blog handle that no longer resolves — renders NOTHING, not
     an empty band with a heading over it. Same `live > 0` guard as
     sh-faq.liquid:63 and sh-reviews.liquid:115.

  5. ONE LINK PER CARD. The heading link is the only anchor in the card; the
     stretched ::after in the stylesheet is what makes the rest of the card
     activate it. Wrapping the whole card in a second <a>, or making the image
     and the "read more" their own anchors, would announce each article three
     times to a screen reader and put three tab stops on one card. The "read
     more" line is therefore aria-hidden — it is a visual affordance for a link
     that is already named by the title.

  6. THE IMAGE IS DECORATIVE BY DEFAULT, AND THAT TAKES A COMPARISON. Shopify
     pre-fills an article image's alt with the article TITLE, so passing
     article.image.alt straight through makes a screen reader announce the
     headline twice on every card — once for the image, once for the link that
     follows it. The alt is emitted only when a merchant actually wrote
     something other than the title.

  7. THE EXCERPT IS strip_html'd, NEVER RAW. article.excerpt is merchant richtext
     and article.content is a whole post — either one emitted raw would drop a
     post's <h2>s, images and scripts into the middle of the product page.

  8. THE DATE IS IN THE META ROW, NOT OVER THE ARTWORK. It was a plaque in the
     image's top-left corner first, and it has to be said plainly why that was
     thrown away: this store's article art is POSTER art with the headline baked
     into the pixels. The plaque landed on the "G" of "Gentle Care for Happier,
     Healthier Skin" on the second card. No corner is safe — the next post could
     put its type anywhere — so the overlay is gone rather than nudged, and the
     image is left to be an image.

  Read time is derived from the post, not typed by anyone: word count / 200,
  floored at 1. It is an estimate and is labelled as one.

  The 4/3 crop is chosen for the same reason as (8). These images are 1402x1122
  and 1024x1024; a 3/2 crop sliced the top off the square one's headline. 4/3 is
  the shallowest ratio that still gives the cards a consistent, non-square
  editorial shape without eating a composition the merchant centred.
{%- endcomment -%}

{{ 'shudhi-journal.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}

{%- assign s = section.settings -%}
{%- assign uid = section.id | default: 'journal' -%}

{%- comment -%} The blog picker hands back a HANDLE, not a blog. An unset picker,
    or one pointing at a blog that has since been deleted, resolves to nothing —
    fall back to the store's default blog rather than rendering an empty band. {%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign jb = blogs[s.blog] -%}
{%- if jb == blank or jb.articles_count == 0 -%}
  {%- assign jb = blogs.news -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%} See (3) and (4). {%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign limit = s.count | default: 3 -%}
{%- assign shown = jb.articles_count | default: 0 -%}
{%- if shown > limit -%}{%- assign shown = limit -%}{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%} `sizes` has to agree with the grid the count modifier actually
    selected, or the browser picks the wrong srcset candidate. With two posts the
    cards are ~40vw, not the 31vw a three-column row would give — declaring 31vw
    there had the browser fetch the 640w file for a 572px slot and go soft on any
    retina screen. Slightly generous on purpose: over-declaring costs bytes,
    under-declaring costs sharpness and cannot be corrected after layout. {%- endcomment -%}
{%- case shown -%}
  {%- when 1 -%}{%- assign img_sizes = '(max-width: 749px) 92vw, 680px' -%}
  {%- when 2 -%}{%- assign img_sizes = '(max-width: 749px) 92vw, 46vw' -%}
  {%- else -%}{%- assign img_sizes = '(max-width: 749px) 92vw, (max-width: 1180px) 46vw, 31vw' -%}
{%- endcase -%}

{%- if shown > 0 -%}
<section class="sh sh-journal sh-journal--n{{ shown }}"
         style="--sh-j-bg:{{ s.bg }};--sh-j-pad-top:{{ s.pad_top }}px;--sh-j-pad-bottom:{{ s.pad_bottom }}px;"
         aria-labelledby="sh-journal-title-{{ uid }}">
  <div class="sh-wrap">

    <div class="sh-journal__head"{% if s.animate %} data-reveal{% endif %}>
      <div class="sh-journal__head-l">
        {%- if s.eyebrow != blank -%}
          <div class="sh-journal__eyebrow sh-eyebrow">
            {{ s.eyebrow }}{% render 'sh-icon', name: 'leaf' %}
          </div>
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- comment -%} h2, not h1: sections/sh-product.liquid owns this page's h1,
            and the FAQ and Reviews bands are both h2 — so the outline stays
            h1 -> h2 -> h3 with the card titles below. {%- endcomment -%}
        <h2 class="sh-journal__title sh-serif" id="sh-journal-title-{{ uid }}">
          {{- s.title | default: 'From the Journal' -}}
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="sh-journal__head-r">
        {%- if s.lede != blank -%}
          <p class="sh-journal__lede">{{ s.lede }}</p>
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- if s.cta_label != blank -%}
          <a href="{{ jb.url }}" class="sh-journal__all sh-btn sh-btn--ghost">
            {{ s.cta_label }} {% render 'sh-icon', name: 'arrow-right' %}
          </a>
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>
    </div>

    <ul class="sh-journal__grid" role="list">
      {%- for article in jb.articles limit: limit -%}

        {%- comment -%} See (7). `default` covers a nil AND an empty excerpt.

            The newline dance is load-bearing, not decoration. strip_html leaves
            the post's paragraph breaks behind as bare newlines, and
            strip_newlines DELETES them rather than collapsing them — so
            "…unwanted energy?\nIn the Indian tradition…" truncated to
            "…unwanted energy?In the Indian tradition…", one run-on word in the
            middle of every excerpt. Converting the breaks to <br /> first gives
            them something to be replaced BY. {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- assign raw = article.excerpt | default: article.content -%}
        {%- assign excerpt = raw | strip_html | newline_to_br
                                 | replace: '<br />', ' ' | replace: '<br/>', ' '
                                 | strip_newlines | truncatewords: 22 -%}

        {%- comment -%} See (6). Shopify DEFAULTS an article image's alt to the
            article title, so the honest-looking `alt="{{ article.image.alt }}"`
            makes a screen reader read the headline twice on every card — once
            as the image, once as the link. Only alt a merchant actually wrote
            survives; the default is dropped back to decorative. {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- assign img_alt = article.image.alt | default: '' -%}
        {%- if img_alt == article.title -%}{%- assign img_alt = '' -%}{%- endif -%}

        {%- comment -%} split/size rather than the number_of_words filter: it
            behaves identically here and needs no filter-availability caveat. {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- assign words = article.content | strip_html | split: ' ' | size -%}
        {%- assign mins = words | divided_by: 200 -%}
        {%- if mins < 1 -%}{%- assign mins = 1 -%}{%- endif -%}

        {%- comment -%} A real tag reads better than the blog name, but every
            article has a blog and not every article has tags. {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if article.tags.size > 0 -%}
          {%- assign label = article.tags.first -%}
        {%- else -%}
          {%- assign label = jb.title -%}
        {%- endif -%}

        <li class="sh-journal__item"{% if s.animate %} data-reveal{% endif %}>
          <article class="sh-journal__card">

            <div class="sh-journal__media{% unless article.image %} sh-journal__media--empty{% endunless %}">
              {%- if article.image -%}
                {%- comment -%} See (6). {%- endcomment -%}
                <img class="sh-journal__img"
                     src="{{ article.image | image_url: width: 900 }}"
                     srcset="{{ article.image | image_url: width: 420 }} 420w,
                             {{ article.image | image_url: width: 640 }} 640w,
                             {{ article.image | image_url: width: 900 }} 900w,
                             {{ article.image | image_url: width: 1300 }} 1300w"
                     sizes="{{ img_sizes }}"
                     alt="{{ img_alt | escape }}"
                     width="900" height="600"
                     loading="lazy" decoding="async">
              {%- else -%}
                {% render 'sh-icon', name: 'shudhi-mark', sw: 1.2 %}
              {%- endif -%}

            </div>

            <div class="sh-journal__text">

              {%- comment -%} See (8). The date lives HERE, not over the artwork.
                  The <time> is emitted whatever the toggles say — a post without
                  a machine-readable date is a post search engines cannot age —
                  and .sh-visually-hidden is position:absolute, so a hidden one
                  adds no flex gap to the meta row. {%- endcomment -%}
              <div class="sh-journal__meta">
                <time class="{% unless s.show_date %}sh-visually-hidden{% endunless %}"
                      datetime="{{ article.published_at | date: '%Y-%m-%d' }}">
                  {{ article.published_at | date: '%d %b' }}
                </time>
                {%- if s.show_date and s.show_tag -%}
                  <span class="sh-journal__dot" aria-hidden="true">&bull;</span>
                {%- endif -%}
                {%- if s.show_tag -%}<span>{{ label }}</span>{%- endif -%}
                {%- if s.show_readtime -%}
                  {%- if s.show_date or s.show_tag -%}
                    <span class="sh-journal__dot" aria-hidden="true">&bull;</span>
                  {%- endif -%}
                  <span>{{ mins }} min read</span>
                {%- endif -%}
              </div>

              {%- comment -%} See (5). {%- endcomment -%}
              <h3 class="sh-journal__h">
                <a class="sh-journal__link" href="{{ article.url }}">{{ article.title }}</a>
              </h3>

              {%- if s.show_excerpt and excerpt != blank -%}
                <p class="sh-journal__excerpt">{{ excerpt }}</p>
              {%- endif -%}

              <span class="sh-journal__more" aria-hidden="true">
                {{ s.more_label | default: 'Read the journal' }}
                {% render 'sh-icon', name: 'arrow-right' %}
              </span>

            </div>
          </article>
        </li>
      {%- endfor -%}
    </ul>

  </div>
</section>
{%- endif -%}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "SH · Recent Journal",
  "class": "index-section",
  "settings": [
    { "type": "text", "id": "eyebrow", "label": "Eyebrow", "default": "From the journal" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "title", "label": "Heading", "default": "Recent from the Journal" },
    { "type": "textarea", "id": "lede", "label": "Supporting line",
      "default": "Notes on herbs, ritual and everyday care — written the way we make the bar: slowly, and by hand." },

    { "type": "header", "content": "Posts" },
    { "type": "blog", "id": "blog", "label": "Blog",
      "info": "Falls back to the store's default blog if this is empty or the blog has no posts." },
    { "type": "range", "id": "count", "label": "Posts to show", "min": 1, "max": 6, "step": 1, "default": 3,
      "info": "Three columns is the layout ceiling. One or two posts get their own narrower grid rather than a stretched card." },
    { "type": "checkbox", "id": "show_date", "label": "Show the date plaque on the image", "default": true },
    { "type": "checkbox", "id": "show_tag", "label": "Show the category label", "default": true,
      "info": "Uses the post's first tag, or the blog name when it has no tags." },
    { "type": "checkbox", "id": "show_readtime", "label": "Show the estimated read time", "default": true },
    { "type": "checkbox", "id": "show_excerpt", "label": "Show the excerpt", "default": true },
    { "type": "text", "id": "more_label", "label": "Card link text", "default": "Read the journal" },

    { "type": "header", "content": "All entries link" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "cta_label", "label": "Link text", "default": "All journal entries",
      "info": "Leave empty to hide it. It always points at the blog chosen above." },

    { "type": "header", "content": "Appearance" },
    { "type": "checkbox", "id": "animate", "label": "Animate on scroll", "default": true },
    { "type": "color", "id": "bg", "label": "Background", "default": "#f2ece0",
      "info": "Keep it light — the text colours are tuned for a light surface. On the product page this sits between the FAQ (#f6ede1) and the reviews (#e9e0cf), so the default is the half-step between them and the page steps cream to deeper cream into the footer." },
    { "type": "range", "id": "pad_top", "label": "Top padding", "min": 0, "max": 140, "step": 4, "default": 84, "unit": "px" },
    { "type": "range", "id": "pad_bottom", "label": "Bottom padding", "min": 0, "max": 140, "step": 4, "default": 92, "unit": "px" }
  ],
  "presets": [ { "name": "SH · Recent Journal" } ]
}
{% endschema %}
